ev('EVD115',"Ch.28 physics claims checked against secondary sources (search snippets): (a) The Alcubierre metric needs negative energy density (exotic matter) and later work (quantum inequalities, 1998) constrains it strongly; so it does not 'prove' earlier FTL objections erroneous, it shows a solution of general relativity that requires matter not known to exist in the needed form (CLM188 disputed). (b) Casimir vacuum energy between plates is a real, measured effect (Lamoreaux 1997), but it is a tiny, boundary-dependent energy difference and does not establish the macroscopic exotic matter the metric needs; the claim that it 'proves' exotic matter exists is overstated, as Hastings's own source Hillman notes (CLM189). (c) Van Den Broeck's 1999 modification cut the requirement drastically (one arXiv paper says to about a solar mass; book says 'a few solar masses') (CLM190). (d) The 2002 paper by 'C.B. Hart et al.' is arXiv gr-qc/0207109 (gr-qc is the arXiv category 'General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology', not a journal); the book's statement that it was published in such a journal, and that Loup's 2003 papers were in General Relativity and Gravitation, I could not confirm (CLM191, CLM192). (e) The Dröscher-Häuser paper is AIAA 2004-3700 and reportedly won a Best Paper award in 2005; New Scientist reported that most physicists could not make sense of Heim theory and engineers judged the proposed experiment infeasible, context the book omits (CLM194). None of this is evidence about UFOs; it concerns the plausibility of interstellar travel.",'TERTIARY_COMPARISON','SRC063; search results','CLM187;CLM188;CLM189;CLM190;CLM191;CLM192;CLM194','21327','MEDIUM','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD116',"Ch.29 checks (search snippets): START II: the Duma approved it on 14 Apr 2000 (not 'kept postponing'), with conditions tied to U.S. ratification of 1997 protocols and ABM preservation; the U.S. never ratified those, so START II never entered into force; the U.S. ABM withdrawal took effect 13 Jun 2002 and Russia announced it was no longer bound on 14 Jun 2002 (book right on the latter). False-alarm incidents: 9 Nov 1979 (alarm lasting six to seven minutes; descriptions of cause differ), 3 and 6 Jun 1980, and 25 Jan 1995 (Norwegian Black Brant XII; one source says about thirty countries were notified vs the book's 35) are all documented in outline; I did not confirm the GAO follow-up, the exact count of notified countries, or Able Archer details. Quotes from Benedict, Cirincione, Kaku, the Guardian article and the 2004/2007 Russian press items were not checked.",'TERTIARY_COMPARISON','SRC064; search results','CLM196;CLM201;CLM202;CLM203','21933','MEDIUM','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD117',"Role of Ch.28-29 in the argument: neither chapter contains an incident or witness account; Ch.28 argues that interstellar travel is not ruled out by physics (a plausibility argument that is neither evidence about any UFO nor accepted as demonstrated by the physics community, per EVD115), and Ch.29 argues that nuclear war remains a live risk, supported mostly by documented false alarms and by quotes from arms-control figures. They therefore add context, not evidence, to the book's central claims.",'ANALYST_ASSESSMENT','book Ch.28-29','CLM195;CLM188','21327','HIGH','N/A')
